>
  <p>
    Spring is the perfect time to clear out the clutter that's accumulated over winter. Whether it's a loft full of forgotten boxes, a garage you can barely walk through, or wardrobes stuffed with clothes you haven't worn in years — a proper spring clean can transform your home and your headspace. Here's our room-by-room guide for Bristol homeowners.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a Plan</h2>
  <p>
    The biggest mistake people make is starting without a plan and running out of energy halfway through. Before you begin, walk through your home and make a list of the areas that need attention. Prioritise the spaces that bother you most. You don't have to do everything in one weekend — spreading it over a few weeks keeps it manageable.
  </p>

  <h2>Room by Room</h2>

  <h3>Bedrooms</h3>
  <p>
    Start with wardrobes and drawers. Use the one-year rule: if you haven't worn it in 12 months, it goes. Sort into keep, donate and bin piles. Bristol has excellent charity shops — <a href="/guides/donate-furniture-bristol/">the SOFA Project, Emmaus and BHF</a> all accept clothing and furniture in good condition. Under-bed storage is a clutter magnet — pull everything out and be ruthless.
  </p>

  <h3>Kitchen</h3>
  <p>
    Expired food, duplicate gadgets, chipped crockery, mismatched containers with missing lids. Kitchens accumulate more unnecessary items per square metre than any other room. Clear out one cupboard at a time. Check under the sink for old cleaning products — these may need <a href="/guides/paint-chemicals-disposal-bristol/">specialist disposal</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Living Room</h3>
  <p>
    Old magazines, DVDs nobody watches, ornaments gathering dust. If it doesn't serve a purpose or bring you genuine pleasure, let it go. This is also a good time to assess your furniture — that sagging sofa or wobbly bookcase you've been tolerating could be replaced. We can collect old <a href="/house-clearance/furniture-removal/">furniture</a> and have it donated or recycled.
  </p>

  <h3>Loft and Garage</h3>
  <p>
    These are often the biggest jobs. Bristol's Victorian and Edwardian terraces have lofts that haven't been cleared in decades. A <a href="/house-clearance/loft-clearance/">professional loft clearance</a> is often the safest and most efficient option — we handle the heavy lifting, the awkward access and the disposal. Garages are similar: years of accumulated tools, paint tins, broken equipment and forgotten boxes.
  </p>

  <h3>Garden and Shed</h3>
  <p>
    Spring is the time to tackle the garden too. Clear winter debris, prune back overgrowth, and sort through the shed. Broken plant pots, rusty tools, old chemicals and bags of hardened compost can all go. Our <a href="/garden-clearance/">garden clearance</a> service handles everything from green waste to shed contents.
  </p>

  <h2>The Three-Box Method</h2>
  <p>
    For each area, use three boxes or bags: <strong>Keep</strong>, <strong>Donate</strong> and <strong>Dispose</strong>. Make decisions quickly — if you deliberate too long over every item, you'll never finish. Items in the "Donate" pile should go to a charity within a week, or they'll drift back into the house. Items in the "Dispose" pile should be cleared promptly.
  </p>

  <h2>When DIY Isn't Enough</h2>
  <p>
    If the volume of clutter feels overwhelming, or if you're dealing with heavy items and awkward access, a professional clearance service can handle it all in a single visit. We clear, carry, load, transport, donate and recycle — you just point at what goes. Call us on <strong>0117 123 4567</strong> or <a href="/contact/">get a free quote</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
